    <span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Pricing</span>
    <h1>Yamaha Mio Rental Price in Hanoi</h1>
    <p>The Yamaha Mio sits in the cheapest automatic tier of our fleet, alongside the Honda Click, and it is the scooter many riders pick when they want twist-and-go simplicity at the lowest verified price. This guide covers the complete Yamaha Mio rental price in Hanoi for every rental period, how the periods compare financially, and who the bike suits best.</p>
    <p>Every figure below comes from the published <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">Hanoi motorbike rental cost</a> hub, which is the single source of verified pricing for the whole fleet. The Mio is one of the automatic options described in the <a href="/hanoi-scooter-rental/">Hanoi scooter rental</a> hub.</p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <p>On this page</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#sec-1">What the Yamaha Mio is</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-2">Yamaha Mio rental prices in Hanoi</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-3">Day, week and month: the arithmetic</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-4">Mio versus Honda Click</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-5">Riding a light automatic in the city</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-6">Practical life with a Mio</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-7">How to book a Yamaha Mio</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <h2 id="sec-1">What the Yamaha Mio is</h2>
    <p>The Mio is Yamaha's light automatic scooter, built for exactly the kind of riding that fills a Hanoi day: short hops, dense traffic, narrow lanes and frequent stops. It has no gears to manage, a simple twist throttle, and a step-through frame that makes mounting easy in street clothes. Around the city you see Mios and similar small automatics everywhere, because the format matches the terrain and the pace of urban life.</p>
    <p>For a visitor, three characteristics matter. It is light, so pushing it into a parking spot or walking it across a crowded stretch takes no effort. It is low and approachable, which helps riders who are short or new to two wheels. And it is mechanically simple, which is a quiet way of saying there is less to go wrong and less to think about. Riders coming from countries where small automatic scooters are normal will feel at home within a minute.</p>
    <p>If you are still mapping Vietnam's bike categories, the short version is this: light 50cc machines in the <a href="/hanoi-50cc-motorbike-rental/">50cc motorbike rental</a> guide, budget automatics like the Mio, larger premium automatics, and semi-automatic underbones like the Honda Wave and Yamaha Sirius. The Mio is the automatic choice at the lowest price point.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-2">Yamaha Mio rental prices in Hanoi</h2>
    <p>The verified Yamaha Mio rental price in Hanoi is 150,000 VND per day, 700,000 VND per week, and between 900,000 and 1,200,000 VND per month. The monthly rate is a confirmed range rather than a single number; the exact figure for your dates is confirmed with us when you book.</p>
    <p>These rates place the Mio in the same tier as the Honda Click, the Honda Wave and the Yamaha Sirius, which makes it one of the four cheapest bikes available. Every number on this page can be cross-checked against the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">full price list</a> before you commit, and that hub also holds the rates for every other model if you want to compare upward.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-3">Day, week and month: the arithmetic</h2>
    <p>Rental period changes the total far more than model choice does. Seven separate daily rentals of the Mio would cost 1,050,000 VND, while the weekly rate is 700,000 VND. Thirty daily rentals would stack to 4,500,000 VND, while the monthly rate sits between 900,000 and 1,200,000 VND, roughly a quarter of the stacked price.</p>
    <p>Per day, the weekly rate works out to 100,000 VND and the monthly range to between 30,000 and 40,000 VND. The pattern is the same across the fleet: anything past a week of riding makes the monthly option the rational choice. The <a href="/monthly-motorbike-rental-hanoi/">monthly motorbike rental in Hanoi</a> hub explains how long-term rentals are structured, what to discuss with us, and how the confirmed monthly ranges apply.</p>
    <p>A practical way to decide is to count your riding days honestly. A ten-day stay with daily riding is already cheaper on a monthly rate than on stacked days, and a month-long stay with even part-time riding is not a close comparison at all. There is no penalty for changing your mind about the period before booking, so tell us your real plan and we will confirm the rate that fits it.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-4">Mio versus Honda Click</h2>
    <p>The Mio and the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/honda-click-rental-price-hanoi/">Honda Click</a> rent for identical verified rates, so the comparison is character rather than cost. Both are light budget automatics with the same day, week and month figures, and both do the same city job with the same twist-and-go manners.</p>
    <p>Riders who weigh the two usually decide on feel. The Click is Honda's take on the format; the Mio is Yamaha's. Sitting on each tells you more than any spec list, and when one is unavailable the other loses you nothing, because the pricing and the riding experience are interchangeable for practical purposes. Availability changes day to day, so ask us what is free on your dates and choose from what is actually in the shop.</p>
    <p>What both bikes share is the honest appeal of the tier: the lowest automatic price in the fleet. If your riding is city-scale and you do not need the extra size of the premium automatics, the Mio tier is the cheapest way to ride without managing gears, and the savings over a month are substantial.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-5">Riding a light automatic in the city</h2>
    <p>Automatic riding removes the gear question entirely: twist the right grip to go, squeeze both levers to stop. That simplicity is why small automatics are the most common first rental in Hanoi. The Mio's lightness helps in the specific situations that define city riding here, such as creeping forward in a crowd of bikes at a light, threading a narrow lane, or stepping off to walk the bike across a busy crossing.</p>
    <p>The habits that keep city riding safe are the same on any bike: ride with the flow rather than against it, brake earlier than the traffic around you expects, keep your eyes moving, and assume you are less visible than you feel. A light scooter makes all of those habits easier to keep, because the bike responds to small inputs instantly. For a fuller primer, browse the <a href="/motorbike-guides/">motorbike guides</a> hub, and for the pickup and inspection process read <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/how-to-rent-a-motorbike-in-hanoi/">how to rent a motorbike in Hanoi</a>.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-6">Practical life with a Mio</h2>
    <p>Day to day, the Mio is an errands-and-exploring machine. It is happiest on city streets and gentle paved routes, and it is the right tool for the loop between your accommodation, the Old Quarter and the lakes. Fuel costs on a small automatic are modest, and fuel stations are dense across the city, so planning around fuel is rarely necessary inside Hanoi; the fuel habits are covered in our general guides.</p>
    <p>Longer paved day trips are possible and pleasant, and riders planning routes out of the city can start from the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-trips/">Hanoi motorbike trips</a> hub. The honest guidance is that the Mio's natural habitat is the city and its near surroundings; if your plans involve sustained high-speed stretches, talk to us about whether a different model fits better before you commit.</p>
    <p>Parking follows the city's normal pattern: use attended parking strips and lots where they exist, take your belongings with you, and keep some small bills for parking. Those customs are described in general terms across our guides, and the <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> hub answers the common rental questions we hear about parking, deposits and conditions.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-7">How to book a Yamaha Mio</h2>
    <p>Call <a href="tel:+84942467674">0942 467 674</a>, email <a href="mailto:nguyentuantu8x@gmail.com">nguyentuantu8x@gmail.com</a>, or visit the shop at 112 Nguyen Van Cu, Long Bien, Hanoi. We are open 09:00&ndash;21:00 daily, and the rental is completed at the shop. Tell us your dates and the period you have in mind, and we will confirm that a Mio is available and quote the exact monthly figure if you are staying long.</p>
    <p>Anything this page has not answered, including deposits, documents and rental conditions, is agreed directly with us at booking, so ask early and get it settled before your first ride. Reconfirm the figures on the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental-cost/">price hub</a> whenever you like, because that page is the complete verified list and this guide deliberately repeats it rather than inventing anything new.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-8">The Mio verdict, and a week in its life</h2>
    <p>Picture a week with the Mio to test whether it fits your trip. Day one is pickup and a careful loop of the neighborhood. Day two takes you across the river or into the center for the first time, and the bike's lightness earns its keep at every light. By day three you are parking without thinking, and by the weekend the scooter has quietly become the way you experience the city, from morning coffee runs to evening loops of the lakes.</p>
    <p>That is the pattern the Mio exists for. It is not the bike for a cross-country tour, and riders planning sustained distance should talk to us about the fleet's other options. But for city-scale exploring at the lowest automatic price in the fleet, it is the straightforward answer, and a month on the confirmed 900,000&ndash;1,200,000 VND range is among the best value-per-dong choices available anywhere in the lineup.</p>
    <p>The decision, stated plainly: if your days are city days and your budget matters, book a Mio or its Honda twin and spend the difference on everything else Hanoi offers. If you want more size and comfort, step up to the premium automatics at 200,000 VND per day, and if you want the cheapest mobility of all with gears, the semi-automatic tier at the same 150,000 VND day rate is there. Whatever you choose, verify the numbers on the price hub, confirm availability with us for your dates, and the bike will do exactly what this guide promised.</p>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions about Yamaha Mio rental</h2>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>How much does a Yamaha Mio rental cost in Hanoi?</h2>
      <p>The verified rates are 150,000 VND per day, 700,000 VND per week and 900,000&ndash;1,200,000 VND per month. The monthly figure is a confirmed range, so contact the shop for the exact price for your dates.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>Is the Yamaha Mio the same price as the Honda Click?</h2>
      <p>Yes. Both light budget automatics rent at the same verified day, week and month rates, so choose between them on feel and availability rather than cost.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>Is the Mio good for a first-time rider?</h2>
      <p>Yes. It is light, low and automatic, with no gears to manage. Most new riders are comfortable within a day of quiet-street practice, and we show every renter the basics before they leave the shop.</p>
    </article>

